Abstract— The AI-controlled Smart IoT-based Greenhouse Monitoring System represents a cutting-edge solution for precision agriculture. Leveraging the power of Artificial Intelligence and the Internet of Things, this system continuously monitors and optimizes environmental variables within the greenhouse, including temperature, humidity, soil moisture, and light levels. Greenhouse gases are the gases in the atmosphere that raise the surface temperature of Earth. Water vapor (H2O), carbon dioxide (CO2), methane (CH4), nitrous oxide (N2O), and Ozone (O3) are some of the examples for greenhouse gases. This article provides an overview of such an automation workflow in greenhouse environments by means of distributed wireless nodes. It makes use of both the IoT and AI technique. Predictions are made through the AI technique which makes use of CNN approach. After a comparative study, sample results from commercial and research greenhouse experiments with the IoT hardware and software have been provided to show connection stability, robustness, and reliability.
Keywords— Greenhouse Gases, Internet of Things, Machineto-Machine (M2M), Wireless Sensor Networks (WSN)
I. INTRODUCTION
Plant growth thrives under the right balance of environmental conditions. Factors like temperature, humidity, and light play a crucial role in a crop’s health and yield. By closely monitoring these variables, growers gain valuable information about their plants’ well-being. This knowledge empowers them to fine-tune the growing environment, ultimately maximizing productivity. By harnessing sunlight and manipulating their environment, greenhouses create havens for plant growth. Constructed with translucent materials like glass or plastic, they capture solar energy, fostering a warm and controlled atmosphere. This allows farmers to extend growing seasons, shield crops from harsh conditions, and cultivate delicate or specialized plants. Greenhouses have become cornerstones of modern agriculture, enabling year-round production of a vast array of fruits, vegetables, flowers, and ornamentals. In colder climates, optimizing the greenhouse microclimate is especially important
for maximizing plant output and minimizing energy use. This fine-tuning can significantly boost crop yields and resource efficiency, proving particularly beneficial for agriculture in northern regions.
The Internet of Things (IoT) has transformed greenhouse management by offering real-time monitoring, automation, and data-driven decision-making. A game-changer in this technology is the ability to harness a vast amount of information from strategically placed sensors. These sensors keep a constant eye on critical environmental aspects like temperature, humidity, light levels, soil moisture, and nutrient content. By gathering and interpreting this data, growers can glean valuable understandings about their crops’ health and development. This empowers them to make informed choices and fine-tune the growing environment for optimal results. Automation is another important benefit of IoT in greenhouses. Connected devices and actuators can automate processes like altering temperature control systems, irrigation, and fertilizer supply using established parameters or real-time data. For example, IoT-enabled irrigation systems may direct the appropriate amount of water to plants depending on soil moisture levels, eliminating water waste and maintaining adequate hydration. Remote monitoring and control are also facilitated by IoT technology. Farmers can access real-time data and control greenhouse operations from anywhere using smartphones, tablets, or computers connected to the internet. This capability enables quick responses to changing conditions, remote troubleshooting, and efficient resource management. Compared to wired systems, Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs) boast several advantages, making them a game- changer in the age of the Internet of Things (IoT) and Machine-to-Machine (M2M) communication. These benefits include high precision data collection, the ability to function even with some sensor failures, easy deployment in diverse settings, affordability, self-sufficiency in terms of power, and resilience in harsh environments. As a result, WSNs are rapidly becoming an essential technology in various fields, including military operations, environmental monitoring, 