IV. EXPERIMENTATION AND RESULTS
An AI-powered greenhouse system takes precision agriculture to new heights. Sensors monitor resource use, and AI optimizes distribution to minimize waste and costs. It also predicts crop yields and analyzes energy use to promote sustainability. AI even detects potential problems early using sensor data, allowing farmers to take swift action. Finally, a mobile app gives growers real-time data for informed decisions.
Smart greenhouses powered by AI and the Internet of Things (IoT) are revolutionizing agriculture by significantly boosting productivity. Real-time data analysis from strategically placed sensors, combined with powerful AI algorithms, allows for precise control over environmental factors. This creates optimal growing conditions for plants, leading to higher yields. The system’s predictive capabilities empower farmers to proactively manage potential problems before they arise, minimizing crop losses and maximizing overall output. Additionally, seamless communication between IoT sensors ensures efficient resource utilization, reducing waste and promoting sustainable practices. The integration of artificial intelligence not only automates routine tasks but also provides valuable insights through data analysis, enabling informed decision- making for farmers. In essence, this technology represents a pivotal advancement in modern agriculture, fostering a more intelligent and resource-conscious approach to greenhouse cultivation. All components are initialized by delivering the necessary electricity. Three sensors, humidity, temperature, and soil moisture are designed to be employed. The Arduino is connected to a cooling fan, a water pump, an LED light source, and a motor pump.
Ethernet is utilized to communicate environmental characteristics with the web interface All environmental parameters are relayed to the server via Ethernet. High temperatures frequently cause crop failure and a decline in fruit yield.
High humidity affects plant photosynthesis, transpiration processes, and nutrient absorption. An overheated greenhouse system causes the soil to quickly dry up, requiring more water to maintain moisture. When the temperature exceeds a certain level, the system turns on the fan which reduces the system inside and when the temperature level is at normal range the fan turns off by itself making it completely automated to use. Moisture sensor monitors the moisture content of the soil. Water is an essential component of plant growth. When the moisture sensor does not detect moisture in the soil, the system activates the motor pump, which supplies water and causes the soil to become moist.
